---
import SectionHeader from '../ui/SectionHeader.astro';
---

<section id="about" class="scroll-mt-20 bg-canvas py-16 sm:py-24">
	<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6">
		<SectionHeader
			align="center"
			eyebrow="About this tool"
			title="A touch screen test that runs online, on any device"
		/>

		<div class="mt-8 space-y-6 text-body-md text-body">
			<p>A touch screen test checks whether your device's screen registers touch accurately, tracks multiple fingers at once, and responds without lag or dead spots. Our touch screen test runs entirely online, right in your browser &mdash; no app to download, no account, and nothing you touch is ever uploaded. Checking a brand-new phone before the return window closes? Diagnosing a cracked screen? This touchscreen test online tool gives you a clear answer in under a minute.</p>

			<div>
				<h3 class="text-display-sm text-ink">Works on iPhone, iPad, Android, and Chromebook</h3>
				<p class="mt-3">This is a touch screen test for iPhone, iPad, and Android phones and tablets alike. Open it in Safari, Chrome, or any modern mobile browser and it just works. Run the <a href="/iphone-touch-screen-test/" class="text-ink underline underline-offset-2 hover:no-underline">iPhone touch screen test</a> to check for ghost touches or a partially unresponsive display. Use the <a href="/ipad-touch-screen-test/" class="text-ink underline underline-offset-2 hover:no-underline">iPad touch screen test</a> to confirm multi-finger gestures track cleanly across the larger screen. Or use it as an <a href="/android-touch-screen-test/" class="text-ink underline underline-offset-2 hover:no-underline">Android touchscreen test</a> to verify your device's <code class="font-mono text-body-sm text-ink">maxTouchPoints</code> and pointer support match the spec sheet. It also doubles as a <a href="/laptop-touch-screen-test/" class="text-ink underline underline-offset-2 hover:no-underline">Chromebook and laptop touchscreen test</a>. Most Chromebooks with touch displays support the same web Touch Events API, so the same suite of tests works without modification.</p>
			</div>

			<div>
				<h3 class="text-display-sm text-ink">A touch screen test app, without the app</h3>
				<p class="mt-3">Most people search for a "touch screen test app android" or a "google play touch screen test app," expecting to install something before they can check their screen. We built this as a touch screen test web app instead. The entire multi-touch, gesture, latency, and dead-zone suite runs as a touchscreen test online, so there's nothing to install, nothing to update, and nothing left behind afterward. If you're comparing the best touchscreen test tools for Android and iOS, a browser-based tool has one advantage an app can't match: instant access. You can run a phone touch screen test on any device the moment you open the link &mdash; even a friend's phone, or one you're about to buy secondhand.</p>
			</div>

			<div>
				<h3 class="text-display-sm text-ink">What the touch screen test actually checks</h3>
				<p class="mt-3">A single "does it work" tap isn't enough to catch most touchscreen problems. This touchscreen test suite breaks the check into seven focused tests. There's multi-touch tracking (how many simultaneous points your screen actually reports), gesture recognition (tap, swipe, pinch, rotate), and a finger-paint canvas for feeling out lag. There's also a tap-accuracy grid that scores your precision, a dead-zone map that reveals unresponsive regions edge to edge, a response-latency test that times the gap between a prompt and your tap, and a scroll test for touch-scrolling smoothness. Together they cover the failure modes a simple online touchscreen test checker misses: ghost touches, ignored fingers, drifting coordinates, and screen areas that quietly stop responding.</p>
			</div>

			<div>
				<h3 class="text-display-sm text-ink">Built for quick answers, not just curiosity</h3>
				<p class="mt-3">Run the full multi touch screen test suite before selling a used phone, after a screen repair, or any time your device feels "off." Everything is detected locally in your browser using standard Touch Events and Pointer APIs, so results reflect your actual hardware and browser &mdash; not a simulation. No signup, no download, and no data leaves your device. Just open the page, tap through the tests, and know exactly how your touchscreen is performing.</p>
			</div>

			<div>
				<h3 class="text-display-sm text-ink">Free, private, and the same test every time</h3>
				<p class="mt-3">This touch screen test stays free with no hidden tier, no ads blocking the test area, and no account wall. It's the same touchscreen test web app whether you open it on a phone one day and an iPad the next, so results are easy to compare across devices. That's useful if you're deciding between two phones, or checking whether a repaired screen matches the original's performance. If you only remember one link, bookmark this one. It works as an iPhone touch screen test, an Android touchscreen test, and an iPad touchscreen test, all from the same page.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
